'''4.''' To appear as if blushing; exhibit a red or roseate hue; bloom freshly or modestly.
{{smaller block|<poem>The sun of heaven, methought, was loth to set,
But stay’d, and made the western welkin ''blush.''</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', K. John, v. 5.}}
Full many a flower is born to ''blush'' unseen.
{{right|''Gray'', Elegy.}}}}
'''5.''' To be ashamed: with ''at'' or ''for.''
{{smaller block|He ''blushes for'' the “disingenuousness of the most devoted worshipper of speculative truth.”
{{right|''Whipple'', Ess. and Rev., I. 19.}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To make red. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Which [blood] . . . ne’er returneth
To ''blush'' and beautify the cheek again.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. VI., iii. 2.}}}}
'''2.''' To express, show, or make known by blushing, or by a change of color similar to a blush. [Rare and poetical.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Pass the happy news,
''Blush'' it thro’ the West.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Maud, xvii.}}}}

'''blush''' (blush), ''n.'' [< ME. ''blusch'', gleam, glimpse; from the verb.] '''1†.''' A gleam.
{{smaller block|To bide a blisful ''blusch'' of the bryȝt sunne.
{{right|''Sir Gawayne and the Green Knight'' (ed. Morris), l. 520.}}}}
'''2.''' A glance; glimpse; look; view: obsolete except in the phrase ''at first blush.''
{{smaller block|''At'' the ''first blush'' we thought they had beene shippes come from France. {{right|''Hakluyt’s Voyages'', III. 336.}}
This sounds, ''at first blush'', very neat, if not even very profound; but a closer examination dissolves it into nothing. {{right|''Bibliotheca Sacra'', XLIII. 618.}}}}
'''3.''' Look; resemblance: as, she has a ''blush'' of

