her father. [North. Eng.] [Hence, collectively, an assembly, company, in the isolated example, ''a blush of boyes'' = a company of boys (“Book of St. Albans”).]—'''4.''' The suffusion of the cheeks or the face with a red color through confusion, shame, diffidence, or the like.
{{smaller block|<poem>If impious acts
Have left thee blood enough to make a ''blush'',
I’ll paint it on thy cheeks.</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'', Spanish Curate, iii. 3.}}
Her ''blush'' of maiden shame. ''Bryant'', Autumn Woods.}}
'''5.''' A red or reddish color; a rosy tint.
{{smaller block|And light’s last ''blushes'' tinged the distant hills.
{{right|''Lord Lyttelton'', Uncertainty, i.}}}}
'''To put to the blush''', {{smaller|to cause to blush or be ashamed.}}

¶
'''blusher''' (blush′ėr), ''n.'' One who blushes, or is given to blushing.
{{smaller block|Mulattoes are often great ''blushers'', blush succeeding blush over their faces.
{{right|''Darwin'', Express. of Emotions, p. 320.}}}}

'''blushet†''' (blush′et), ''n.'' [< ''blush'' + ''-et''.] A little blusher; a modest young girl.
{{smaller block|Go to, little ''blushet''. ''B. Jonson'', Entertainments.}}

'''blushful''' (blush′fu̇l), ''a.'' [< ''blush'' + ''-ful''.] Full of blushes.
{{smaller block|<poem>From his [the sun’s] ardent look the turning Spring
Averts her ''blushful'' face. ''Thomson'', Summer, l. 7.

The true, the ''blushful'' Hippocrene.</poem>
{{right|''Keats'', Ode to Nightingale.}}}}

'''blushfully''' (blush′fu̇l-i), ''adv.'' With many blushes.

