'''bo<sup>2</sup>''' (bō), ''interj.'' [Also written ''boh'' and formerly also ''boe''; a mere exclamation. Cf. D. “''hij kan'' boe ''noch'' ba ''zeggen'',” equiv. to E. “he cannot say ''bo'' to a goose.” Cf. ''boo''<sup>1</sup>.] An exclamation used to inspire surprise or fright; especially, a cry uttered by children to frighten their fellows. Also ''boo''.
{{smaller block|I’ll rather put on my flashing red nose and my flaming face, and come wrapped in a calf’s skin, and cry ''bo, bo!'' I’ll fray the scholar, I warrant thee.
{{right|''Old Play'', Wily Beguiled.}}}}
'''Not able to say bo! to a goose''', {{smaller|very foolish or timid.}}

'''b. o.''' A common abbreviation in stock-exchange reports and documents of ''buyer’s option'': as, ''b. o.'' 3 (that is, at the buyer’s option within 3 days).

